s any retained children (i.e., children that are not removed) cannot be removed.\n\nThus algorithm: Build tree from parent links. Maintain set of retained node IDs initially all. Then iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID. For each node that is removable, not root, not required symbol (check symbol in required_symbols), and all its children (in the original tree) are either not retained (i.e., already removed) or are also being removed in this iteration? Actually careful: We need to check at the time of removal whether all children are or will be removed? Since we are processing in depth order (deepest first), children will be processed before parents. So when we consider a parent, all its children have already been decided (either removed or retained). So we can check: if any child of this node is still in retained set, then we cannot remove this node. If all children are removed (i.e., not in retained set), then we can consider removal. Also we need to check required symbols: after removal, the set of symbols among retained nodes (excluding this node) must still include all required_symbols. That means if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it anyway. Also need to ensure root not removed.\n\nAdditionally, what about nodes that have no children? They are leaves; if removable and symbol not required, we can remove them if required symbols still present.\n\nBut also consider \"utility\"? The description mentions \"utility\" but not in rules. Possibly not used.\n\nOutput: removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list? The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (probably alphabetically? Or by depth? It says \"sorted retained IDs\" likely by ID string. And removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (i.e., the order we decided to remove them). In our iteration, we process nodes in depth descending then ID ascending, and we decide to remove some. That order is the removal order.\n\nThus implementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: case is a dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Nodes is list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility.\n2. Build a dict mapping node id to node info.\n3. Build children mapping from parent to list of child ids.\n4. Initialize retained = set of all node ids.\n5. Initialize removed_order = [].\n6. Create list of nodes to consider: for each node in nodes that is removable and id != \"root\"? Actually root might not be removable, but check removable flag. But also node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. So filter: if not node['removable'] or node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip. But also root? root has removable false anyway. So we only consider nodes that are removable and symbol not required.\n7. Sort this list by (-depth, id) so deepest first, then alphabetical id for equal depth.\n8. For each node in sorted list:\n   - Check if node id is still in retained (might have been removed as descendant? Actually descendant removal happens before parent, but if we are processing deeper nodes first, and we remove a node, its parent will later be processed. So parent might still have children retained? But if we remove a child, we remove it from retained set. So when we get to parent, its children (the removed ones) are no longer in retained; good.\n   - Check if all children of this node (from original tree) are NOT in retained. i.e., for each child in children mapping, if child is in retained, then cannot remove because it has retained children. This ensures we only remove if all descendants have already been removed.\n   - Tentatively remove: compute new retained set = retained - {node_id}. Then check that the set of symbols of retained nodes (including node's symbol? No, after removal) still contains all required_symbols. If yes, then actually remove: update retained = new retained; append node_id to removed_order.\n9. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (by string ID).\n10. Return dict with \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order (order of removal) and \"retained_node_ids\": sorted retained IDs.\n\nBut note: The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We already do that by sorting by (-depth, id). Also: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We check that.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires arrays of strings. So we sort retained IDs by string.\n\nEdge cases: Nodes with no children (leaves) will have empty children list, so condition passes. But if leaf node is removable and symbol not required, check required symbols after removal. However, if leaf node's symbol is the only occurrence of a required symbol, then removal would lose required symbol, so we keep it. That's correct.\n\nWhat about nodes that are not removable? They stay.\n\nWhat about nodes that are removable but have some children that are also removable but not yet processed? Since we process deeper first, all children deeper than parent will be processed before parent. So when we reach parent, all its descendants have either been removed or retained. If any descendant is retained, then parent has retained children -> cannot remove. So that's fine.\n\nWe need to be careful: The required symbols list may contain symbols that appear multiple times. Removing one occurrence is okay as long as at least one remains. So check that after removal, for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent_id = node['parent']\n        if parent_id is not None:\n            children[parent_id].append(node['id'])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    # root is probably 'root' but we don't remove it anyway.\n    # Build list of candidates: removable and symbol not required\n    candidates = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['removable'] and node['symbol'] not in required_symbols:\n            candidates.append(node)\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_order = []\n    for node in candidates:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check that all children are not retained (i.e., already removed)\n        all_children_removed = all(child not in retained for child in children[nid])\n        if not all_children_removed:\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove, check required symbols\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\n        # Check if each required symbol still present in new_retained\n        symbols_present = {node_map[nid2]['symbol'] for nid2 in new_retained}\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_present):\n            retained = new_retained\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut wait: The root node (id \"root\") has removable false, so it's not in candidates. So fine.\n\nAlso, nodes with required symbols are not candidates, so they stay.\n\nCheck first fixture: nodes: root (depth0, removable false, symbol root). n1 depth1 removable false symbol s1 (required), so stays. n2 depth1 removable true symbol s2 not required. n3 depth1 removable false symbol s0. n4 depth2 removable true symbol s1 (required? Actually required symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]. n4 has symbol s1, so it is required and removable true but symbol is required, so we treat it as not candidate. So n4 will not be removed. n5 depth2 removable false symbol s2. n6 depth2 removable true symbol s0 not required. So candidates: n2, n6. Depth descend: n6 depth2 > n2 depth1. So process n6 first. n6 leaf, children none. Check required: after removing n6, symbols still include root, s1 (n1 and n4), s0? s0 from n3? n3 is retained, s0 present but not required. So required_symbols satisfied. Remove n6. Then n2: children: n4? n4 is retained (symbol s1 required). So child n4 is retained, so all_children_removed is False -> skip n2. So removed: [\"n6\"], retained: all others. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is this the expected behavior? The problem statement mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"interestingness oracle\". It could be that we need to consider if the reduction is \"interesting\" i.e., the resulting tree still satisfies some property. But the fixtures don't provide an oracle function. Possibly we assume that any reduction that preserves required symbols is \"interesting\"? Or perhaps the \"utility\" field is used for something like \"interestingness\"? But description says \"while preserving an interestingness oracle\" but then \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" No explanation of utility.\n\nMaybe the task is simpler: We just need to remove as many removable nodes as possible subject to constraints (root, required symbols, children retained). The candidates order depth-first then ID, and condition that all children must be removed (i.e., if a node has any retained child, it can't be removed). This is typical in tree reduction algorithms like Perses.\n\nBut there is also mention of \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Maybe we need to ensure that after removal, the tree is still a valid parse tree under some grammar? Since we don't have grammar, maybe we assume that if we remove a node, we also remove its subtree, and that is valid because the parent's symbols can appear without the child? In a parse tree, removing an internal node would break the rule. Perses often removes nodes and \"replaces\" them with their children if possible. But the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids; no replacement or restructuring. So likely we just remove entire subtrees (i.e., if a node is removed, all its descendants are removed regardless of whether they are removable). But our algorithm only removes nodes that have all children already removed. That means we only remove nodes if the entire subtree is removed. So effectively, we remove entire subtrees rooted at some node, but only if we can remove all nodes in that subtree. This is like \"delete node and its descendants\". That is a common reduction.\n\nHowever, note that we only remove nodes that are removable. Some nodes may not be removable, so they block removal of their ancestors. So if a non-removable node exists in a subtree, the entire subtree cannot be removed because that non-removable node cannot be removed. So our algorithm correctly handles that: if a node has a non-removable child, that child remains retained, so parent cannot be removed because it has a retained child.\n\nAlso, we check that required symbols are preserved. Since we only remove subtrees, if a required symbol node is removed, the required symbol may be lost. But we explicitly don't remove nodes with required symbols. So they remain.\n\nThus the algorithm seems correct.\n\nBut we need to ensure that we do not remove nodes that have children that are not removed (i.e., retained). That is exactly our condition.\n\nNow, what about the order: we process deeper first. For a leaf node that is removable and not required, we can remove it. Then its parent might become eligible if all children are removed. This matches depth-first bottom-up removal.\n\nNow, consider the output format: removed_node_ids is the order of removal. In our loop, we append in the order we process and decide to remove. That order will be depth descending, then ID ascending because we iterate in that order. For nodes at same depth, ID ascending. That's fine.\n\nRetained_node_ids sorte